// A method's OWN type parameter that shadows one of the enclosing type's is a different `K`, and
// it resolves through a different channel: the receiver's reflected tag records the CLASS's
// arguments, the call node's `type_args` operand carries the METHOD's. Both reach the one compiled
// body, so the shadowing name answers with the method's instantiation — here `A`, inferred from
// the argument — and the class's `Pair<A, int>` is simply not what `type_name::<K>()` asks about.
//
// This was a refusal (E0058) while a method had no forwarding channel at all: the two `K`s were
// indistinguishable at run time, so answering either would have been a guess. Shadowing is now an
// ordinary scoping question with an ordinary answer, and the sibling case that still refuses —
// a parameter no call can infer — is `reflection/type_name_diagnostics.noe`.
// expect: stdout "1A"
// expect: exit 0
struct A {
    x: int
}
class Pair<K, V> {
    pub n: int

    fn new(n: int): Pair<K, V> {
        return Pair { n: n };
    }

    fn shadowed<K>(x: K): string {
        return "${self.n}" ~ type_name::<K>();
    }
}
p: Pair<A, int> = Pair.new(1);
echo p.shadowed(A { x: 2 });
